<section xml:id="section-groups-definition-examples">
  <title>Groups - Definition and Examples</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-groups-definition">
    <p>
      Abstract algebra is built by taking familiar mathematical
      structure and, well, abstracting them: pulling the details of
      their structure <mdash/> their patterns, their arrangements
      <mdash/> away from their particulars and trying to understand
      their general patterns. We start with number sets, which are the
      most familiar. What do number sets have? Well, a good place to
      start is arithmetic: they have binary operations with certain
      structure and patterns. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Most number sets have multiple binary operations: addition,
      subtraction, multiplication, division. We'll get to abstractions
      with multiple binary operations interacting, but it makes sense
      to start at a more fundamental place. Therefore, let's just
      focus on one binary operation. We can think of the integers with
      just addition. We can think of the positive real numbers with
      just multiplication. These are sets with one binary operation. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This leads to the definition of a group. A group is the first
      and most fundamental abstraction of a set with a binary
      operation. Here are the details.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A set <m>G</m> with a binary operation <m>\cdot</m> is a
          <term>group</term> if it satisfies the following properties. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              The binary operation is associative.
            </li>
            <li>
              There exsts an element <m>e \in G</m> which is an
              identity for the binary operation.
            </li>
            <li>
              For each element <m>g \in G</m>, there exist an element
              <m>g^{-1} \in G</m> which is an inverse to <m>g</m>:
              that is, <m>g \cdot g^{-1} = g^{-1} \cdot g = e</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
          Since both the set and the binary operation are required for
          the definition, the formal way to refer to a group would be
          to write <m>(G, \cdot)</m>. Very often, the binary operation
          is understood from the context and we just use the set
          <m>G</m> to refer to the group. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      That's all we need: associativity, identity and inverses. In
      particular, we do not assume that the binary operation is
      commutative. If it is, we have a term for this. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a group. If the binary operation is
          commutative, we say that <m>G</m> is a
          <term>commutative</term> group or an <term>abelian</term>
          group.  If the binary operation is not commutative, then
          <m>G</m> is a <term>noncommuative</term> or
          <term>nonabelian</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Groups are sets and sets have cardinality: how many elements are
      in the set. For groups, this gets a special term. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>order</term> of a group is its cardinality as a
          set. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      As discussed in <xref ref="definition-sets-basics"/>, the only
      cardinalities that I will deal with in this course are finite
      cardinalities (<m>n \in \NN</m>), countably infinite, and
      uncountably infinite. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Before moving on to the first examples, let me make a very
      important point about notation. I've defined a group using
      <sq><m>\cdot</m></sq> as the generic symbol for a binary
      operation. This is fine for the definition, but now that we know
      about abelian and non-abelian groups, there are important
      notation conventions. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Abelian groups are very often written using <sq>+</sq> as the
      binary operation. In group theory (and elsewhere throughout
      mathematics), addition is treated as the archetypical
      commutative operation, and we will often try to write abelian
      groups using addition notation, even if this means extending the
      normal notion of addition. The point here is that the symbol
      <sq>+</sq> is very strongly associated with commutative
      operations <mdash/> when you see addition, you should think
      commutativity. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Similarly, nonabelian groups are almost always written with some
      kind of multiplicative notation. The most common notation here
      is to drop the symbol entirely: if <m>g, h \in G</m> are group
      elements, then usually the operation is just written <m>gh</m>.
      This will be the convention that I stick to in these notes. When
      you see this notation, you should have the possibility of a
      noncommutative operation in mind. That said, many
      multiplications are still commutative, so there are no
      guarantees here in either direction. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-groups-examples">
    <title>First Examples of Groups</title>
    <p>
      Examples are, of course, very important to understanding what is
      going on with this abstract definition. Let's go through a bunch
      of examples. In addition to these examples, the next three
      section of this chapter are all devoted to another important
      example of a group. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Usual Sets of Numbers</title>
      <p>
        Many of the usual numbers sets are groups. That said, not all
        of them are groups, and such a fact always depends on the
        binary operation.
        <ul>
          <li>
            The natural numbers <m>\NN</m> are not a group under any
            of the normal arithmetic operations. The problem here is
            inverses: the inverse under addition of <m>n</m> should be
            <m>-n</m>, but the natural numbers don't have negatives.
            Similarly, the inverse for multiplication would be
            <m>\frac{1}{n}</m>, which is also not a natural number. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The integers are a group under addition. The addition of
            two integers is an integer, the number <m>0</m> is the
            identity for addition, and the inverse of <m>n</m> is 
            <m>-n</m>, which remains in the integers. The integers are
            not a group under multiplication, again since
            <m>\frac{1}{n}</m> is not an integer.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rational numbers <m>\QQ</m> are a group under
            addition: <m>0</m> is still the identity and negatives
            still give the inverses. The rational numbers are not a
            group under multiplication because there is no inverse
            for <m>0</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rational number except for zero, usually written
            <m>\QQ^*</m>, are a group under multiplication. The
            identity is <m>1</m>, and the inverse of
            <m>\frac{a}{b}</m> is <m>\frac{b}{a}</m>. This inverse
            always works because I have excluded zero. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The real numbers are a group under addition. They are not
            a group under multiplication again because <m>0</m> has no
            inverse. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The real numbers except for zero, usually written
            <m>\RR^*</m>, are a group under multiplication.
          </li>
          <li>
            The complex numbers are a group under addition. <m>0</m>
            (or, more formally, <m>0 + 0 \imath</m>) is the identity.
            The inverse of <m>a + b\imath</m> is <m>a - b\imath</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The complex numbers without zero are a group under
            multiplication. The identity is <m>1</m> (or <m>(1 + 0
            \imath)</m>) and the inverse of <m>a + b\imath</m> is
            <m>\frac{a - b\imath}{a^2 + b^2}</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        All of these number groups are abelian: addition and
        multiplication for numbers is always commutative. 
      </p>
      <p>
        I've only talked about addition and multiplication here.
        Subtraction and division are also binary operations, but not
        really the kind of binary operations that lend themselves to
        group structure. (They are neither associative or commutative,
        in particular). When thinking about number sets as groups,
        only addition and multiplication should really be considered.
        You should think of subtraction as something relatve to the
        inverse operation: subtraction is adding an inverse. In this
        way, subtraction is part of the group structure, but only
        because it comes from addition and inverses. Likewise,
        division is only multiplication by an inverse as well. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="example-integers-modulo">
      <title>Integers Modulo <m>n</m></title>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="definition-integers-mod"/>, I defined the
        integers mod <m>n</m>, written <m>\ZZ/m\ZZ</m>. This is also a
        group under addition. In this case, I am adding
        equivalence classes, since <m>\ZZ/m\ZZ</m> consists of
        equivalent classes. There are a number of ways to write this,
        but the follow two are the most common. For example, let's
        work in <m>\ZZ/13\ZZ</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \overline{11} + \overline{9} = \overline{7} 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            11 + 9 \equiv 7 \mod 13 
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The integers modulo n are not a group under multiplication.
        Even with equivalence classes, there is still no inverse to
        the class <m>\bar{0}</m>. However, a subset can be made into a
        group by multiplication. I need a result from integer
        arithmetic here, which I will present without proof. 
      </p>
      <proposition>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The element <m>\bar{k}</m> of <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> has a
            multiplicative inverse if and only if <m>\gcd(n,k) =
            1</m>. The number of such classes (which is equivalent to
            the positive integers between <m>2</m> and <m>n</m> which
            are coprime to <m>n</m>) defines a function called
            <term>Euler's totient</term> function, and is written
            <m>\phi(n)</m>. 
          </p>
        </statement>
      </proposition>
      <p>
        Like with numbers sets above, a superscript star is used to
        indicate the elements with multiplicative inverses:
        <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ^*</m>. This is a group under multiplication and
        the size of this group is <m>\phi(n)</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      These are the first example of finite groups: <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>
      has only <m>n</m> elements; <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ^*</m> has only
      <m>\phi(n)</m> elements. For finite groups, since there are
      only finitely many elements and therefore finitely many pairs
      of elements, the binary operations can be displayed (or even
      defined) explicitly by showing all the possibilities. One way
      to do this is with a <sq>multiplication table</sq>: a table
      that shows all possible operations. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a finite group. A table which shows all of
          the outcomes of the binary operation for <m>G</m> is called
          a <term>Caley table</term> for the group. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Caley tables are a very nice way to see the explicit structure
      of a group, at least for small groups. I'll make frequent use of
      them to talk about groups structure. 
    </p>
    <p>
      For now, let me show you some Caley tables for the integers
      modulo n. For all of these, feel free to verify the operations
      yourself. 
    </p>
    <example xml:id="example-caley-z20-units">>
      <p>
        Here is the Caley table for <m>\ZZ/7\ZZ</m> under the
        operation of addition. I'll not write the bars over the
        numbers, but these do, of course, represent equivalence
        classes modulo 7. 
      </p>
      <me>
        \begin{array}{c| c c c c c c c }
          + \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\
          \hline
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \\
          1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\
          2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
          3 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
          4 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
          5 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\
          6 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \\
        \end{array}
      </me>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Here is the Caley table for <m>\ZZ/20\ZZ^*</m> under the
        operation of multiplication. There are eight numbers coprime
        to <m>20</m>, so those numbers are the classes in this group. 
      </p>
      <me>
        \begin{array}{c| c c c c c c c c }
          \times \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \amp 9 \amp 11 \amp 13 \amp 17 \amp
          19  \\
          \hline
          1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \amp 9 \amp 11 \amp 13 \amp 17 \amp
          19 \\
          3 \amp 3 \amp 9 \amp 1 \amp 7 \amp 13 \amp 19 \amp 11 \amp
          17 \\
          7 \amp 7 \amp 1 \amp 9 \amp 3 \amp 17 \amp 11 \amp 19 \amp
          13 \\
          9 \amp 9 \amp 7 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 19 \amp 17 \amp 13 \amp
          11 \\
          11 \amp 11 \amp 13 \amp 17 \amp 19 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \amp
          9 \\
          13 \amp 13 \amp 19 \amp 11 \amp 17 \amp 3 \amp 9 \amp 1 \amp
          7 \\
          17 \amp 17 \amp 11 \amp 19 \amp 13 \amp 7 \amp 1 \amp 9 \amp
          3 \\
          19 \amp 19 \amp 17 \amp 13 \amp 11 \amp 9 \amp 7 \amp 3 \amp
          1 \\
        \end{array}
      </me>
    </example>
    <p>
      Before moving on, there are a couple important things to note
      about Caley tables.
      <ul>
        <li>
          The two examples we gave were for abelian groups, so
          these tables are symmetric over the diagonal. You may
          remember from elementary school multiplication tables that
          usually half the table was left blank for this reason.
          However, groups in general are not abelian, so I will
          write the entire table.
        </li>
        <li>
          Since groups are not commutative, we need to be explicit for
          the order of multiplication. If the row is labelled <m>g</m>
          and the column is labelled <m>h</m>, then the entry in that
          square is <m>gh</m>, not <m>hg</m>. 
        </li>
        <li>
          You may have noticed that each row and each column has
          exactly one of each group elements. This is always true, and
          a good way to check if you have made a correct Caley table. 
        </li>
        <li>
          Finally, the Caley table shows inverses as well. In the
          table for <m>\ZZ/20\ZZ^*</m>, I can read off the table that
          the inverse of 13 is 17, for example. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now we can move on from numbers. There are many more
      mathematical objects that we can make groups out of. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Polynomial Groups</title>
      <p>
        The set of polynomials in a variable <m>x</m> with
        coefficients from a particular set of numbers forms a group
        under addition. The <m>0</m> polynomial is the identity,
        and the inverse of <m>p(x)</m> is <m>-p(x)</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>Groups of Functions</title>
      <p>
        The functions in calculus have the usual arithmetic
        operations: I can add, multiply, subtract and divide
        functions. Therefore, I can form groups out of them. There are
        many such groups.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>C(\RR)</m> is the set of continuous function <m>f: \RR
            \rightarrow \RR</m>. This forms a group under addition.
            The zero function is the identity (the function that sends
            everything to zero). The inverse of a function <m>f(x)</m>
            is the function <m>-f(x)</m>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            I can multiply functions, but <m>C(\RR)</m> is not a group
            under multiplication because of the potential for division
            by zero. However, I can still make a group here. The set
            of <term>nowhere-zero</term> functions <m>f: \RR
            \rightarrow \RR</m> is the set of function where the
            output is never zero. (Equivalently, I could say that
            these are function <m>f: \RR \rightarrow \RR^*</m>). This
            set does form a group under multiplication. The identity is
            the function which is constant 1 (every output is the
            number 1). The inverse of <m>f(x)</m> is
            <m>\frac{1}{f(x)}</m>, which is well-defined because the
            functions are all nowhere-zero. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The previous definition had continuous function whose
            domain was all real numbers. By altering the domain or the
            condition, I can make all sorts of examples. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of continuous real-valued function on a closed
            interval, written <m>C([a,b])</m> is a group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of continuously differentiable real-valued
            functions on <m>\RR</m>, written <m>C^1(\RR)</m>, is a
            group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of integrable real-valued functions on <m>\RR</m>
            is group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of continuously twice differentiable real-valued
            functions on <m>\RR</m>, written <m>C^2(\RR)</m>, is a
            group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of analytic real-valued
            functions on <m>\RR</m>, written <m>C^\infty(\RR)</m>, is a
            group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of nowhere-zero real-valued differentiable
            function on the positive real numbers is a group under
            multiplication.
          </li>
          <li>
            The set of positive real-valued increasing functions on
            <m>\RR</m> is a group under addition. 
          </li>
          <li>
            I could go on, but you hopefully get the idea. Many
            conditions and many choices of domain give way to groups of
            functions. Note that all of the groups in this section are
            still abelian groups, since the addition and
            multiplication are still commuttive. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        The previous example covered functions under pointwise
        operations: addition or multiplication of functions. But
        functions have another binary operation: composition. I can
        also make groups of functions under composition. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be any set. Then the set of bijective function
        <m>f: A \rightarrow A</m> forms a group under composition. The
        identity of this group is the identity function <m>\Id_A</m>,
        which was defined in <xref
        ref="definition-identity-function"/>.  Since all the functions
        here are bijective, they are all invertible; the inverse of
        <m>f</m> is the inverse function <m>f^{-1}</m>, as discussed
        in <xref ref="definition-functions-jective"/>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This group of bijective functions can be a finite or infinite
        groups, depending on the set <m>A</m>. However, this is almost
        always a nonabelian group. Usually, composition functions
        depends very much on the order of the composition:
        interchanging the order leads to very different composition. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>Boolean Groups</title>
      <p>
        This may seem like a strange example, but it is a useful one.
        The set <m>\{T, F\}</m> of truth-values in logic is a group
        under two different operations. First, it is a group under the OR
        operation; under this operation, <m>F</m> is the identity.
        Second, it is also a group under the AND operation; under this
        operation, <m>T</m> is the identity. Here are the Caley tables
        for these two groups. 
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c| c c }
            OR \amp T \amp F \\
            \hline
            T \amp T \amp T\\
            F \amp T \amp F \\
          \end{array}
          \hspace{1cm}
          \begin{array}{c| c c }
            AND \amp T \amp F \\
            \hline
            T \amp T \amp F\\
            F \amp F \amp F \\
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      That ends this list of first examples. However, the next three
      sections of these notes are each devoted to another important
      class of groups, so we are far from done with examples. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I'll end with two silly examples, but there are also important.
      Mathematicians love trivial cases, and getting the trivial cases
      right often makes a difference for proofs. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Any singleton set <m>\{e\}</m> is a group with identity
        <m>e</m> and binary operation <m>e \cdot e = e</m>. There is
        almost nothing to check here, but you could go through the
        properties carefully if you wish and check what little there
        is here to check. (For example, the inverse of <m>e</m> is
        <m>e</m>, since <m>e \cdot e = e</m>, which covers all the
        inverses needed since there is only one element.) 
      </p>
      <p>
        A singleton may be a group, but the empty set <m>\emptyset</m>
        is not a group under any binary operations. The reason that
        <m>\emptyset</m> is not a group is because of the properties:
        the second property in the definition of a group says that an
        identity must exist. The empty set has no elements at all, so
        nothing to serve as an identity. Therefore, it cannot be a
        group.  
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
